+Empirically, $c$ is far from zero throughout long sequences: $\bar{c}\in[0.62, 0.77]$ on TUM and ScanNet (\cref{tab:drift}). In this practical regime $c{>}0$, both gates are monotone-decreasing in $c$ and pull the update toward a smaller magnitude. Under the single-frame setup above, $\tau{=}1$ makes the two gates nearly coincide over this range, with $g^{\text{DDD3R}}\in[0.32, 0.39]$ from \cref{eq:effgate} and $g^{\text{brake}}\in[0.32, 0.35]$, a gap of at most 13\%. They also share the value $0.5$ at $c{=}0$ and separate only toward pure drift, where $g^{\text{DDD3R}}(1){=}\alpha_\parallel{=}0.05$ while $g^{\text{brake}}(1){=}\mathrm{sigmoid}(-1){\approx}0.27$. So in the actual operating regime brake applies nearly the same update magnitude as DDD3R, with three caveats below.
 
 \paragraph{Three losses of the relaxation.}
 \textbf{(L1) Asymmetric handling of anti-aligned deltas.} For $c{<}0$ (rare but possible during scene transitions), DDD3R still suppresses the drift component to $\alpha_\parallel\|\boldsymbol{\delta}_t\||c|$, while brake \emph{boosts} the update via $\mathrm{sigmoid}(+\tau|c|){>}\tfrac{1}{2}$. \textbf{(L2) No directional separation.} Brake produces a single per-token scalar that multiplies the entire $\boldsymbol{\delta}_t$, so it cannot suppress drift while preserving novelty within the same delta; DDD3R applies $\alpha_\parallel{<}\alpha_\perp$ separately along $\boldsymbol{\delta}_t^{\parallel}$ and $\boldsymbol{\delta}_t^{\perp}$. \textbf{(L3) Single-frame history.} Brake compares only consecutive deltas, while DDD3R's drift direction is an EMA over the full history; in scenes where underlying drift varies slowly but per-frame deltas are noisy, EMA cancels the noise and brake does not.
