 \paragraph{Practical recommendations.} The experiments yield a clear deployment guideline.
 \textbf{(i) \dddbrake{} is the most robust default}, ranking 1st on ScanNet 1000f (relpose), Bonn and Sintel (vdepth), and 7Scenes (recon) without any directional decomposition.
 \textbf{(ii) \dddortho{} gives an additional 10--15\% on low-drift indoor scenes} (TUM and similar slow-scanning settings), where drift mostly reflects harmful error accumulation.
-\textbf{(iii) Default \dddortho{} ($\gamma{=}0$) is the best aggregate for outdoor driving.} KITTI Odometry is also our strongest test of domain shift, since the checkpoint is trained on short indoor-heavy pairs while these are outdoor driving sequences of 271--4661 frames. \dddortho{} reduces ATE by $20.1\%$ over CUT3R across the 11 sequences, and on the official segment-based metrics it reduces translation error by $7.6\%$ and rotation error by $58.6\%$, and per-sequence optima drift toward mild $\gamma\!\in\![0.5,2]$ (\cref{tab:spectrum}). Switching between operating points changes only scalar hyperparameters.
+\textbf{(iii) On outdoor driving, ATE does not separate the operating points.} Across the 11 KITTI Odometry sequences (271--4661 frames), \dddortho{} has the lowest ATE, $21.1\%$ below CUT3R, but its lead over TTT3R, \dddconst{} and \dddbrake{} is not significant ($p{\geq}0.17$, Wilcoxon over sequences). On the KITTI segment-based metrics, computed after Sim(3) alignment and averaged over sequences, it reduces translation error by $24.6\%$ and rotation error by $46.6\%$ over CUT3R, and its rotation error is significantly lower than that of CUT3R, TTT3R, \dddconst{} and \dddbrake{} ($p{\leq}0.019$). This gain comes at a frame-level cost, since its translational RPE is $2.8$ times that of CUT3R. Absolute errors stay large for every method, with translation error above $50\%$, so these are relative gains in a setting that none of them handles well. Switching between operating points changes only scalar hyperparameters.
 
 \paragraph{Beyond this backbone.} Two further studies are in the appendix. On \textbf{ARKitScenes} (\cref{app:arkit}), 18 real-world handheld scenes, drift energy measured before any pose evaluation correctly predicted which operating point would fail. On \textbf{Point3R}~\cite{point3r}, a different backbone with an explicit pointer memory (\cref{app:crossbackbone}), the magnitude fix gives $5.4\%$ on ScanNet at the same $\alpha{=}0.5$, while its low drift energy correctly indicates that directional decomposition should not be used there.
 
